{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nrebar is a concurrency-heavy project (multi-process writers over a git-backed store, file locks, detached children, CI-level push contention), which makes it both the ideal dogfood target and the worst case for trigger over-fire. Measured over the last 300 non-merge `origin/main` commits by replaying each commit's diff through `content_triggered_overlays` (committed-only vs. with the noisy tier; reproducible — the measurement script is recorded on the session log and in the commit message): the committed high-precision tier fires on 13.3% of diffs (40/300); the noisy tier (`.lock`, `retry`, `Popen`, `fork`, …) adds a further 22.0% (66/300), for 35.3% combined. Three of the four traced corpus bugs (`fine-wisteria-solenodon` via `index.lock`, `fervid-boring-python` via `retry`, `eclectic-spotted-barb` via `Popen`/`start_new_session`) trigger only through noisy-tier vocabulary — so for THIS repo the noisy tier is load-bearing, and per the parent epic's decided design it lives in the project overlay, not the committed list. Nothing currently documents the two-layer trigger design for other projects to copy.\n\n## Approach\n\nTwo deliverables. (1) Add a `concurrency` entry to the `code_review` section of this repo's `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` using the project-extension mechanism delivered by the prerequisite story: `trigger_tokens` `[\".lock\", \"retry\", \"Popen\", \"start_new_session\", \"setsid\", \"fork\", \"index.lock\", \"stage_and_commit\", \"push_tickets_branch\", \"refs/reconciler/\"]` and `applies_to` `[\"**/_store/**\", \"**/lock*\", \"**/enrich_drain*\", \".github/workflows/**\"]`. (2) Document the two-layer trigger design in `docs/llm-framework.md` adjacent to the existing code-review routing material (the routing/prompt/eval pointer block around docs/llm-framework.md:661): the committed high-precision tier and its survey grounding, the project noisy tier and how a project adds vocabulary, the measured fire-rate tradeoff (13.3% committed vs +22.0% noisy on this repo, with the reproducible measurement method), and this repo's entry as the worked example. Positive rationale: the dogfood entry is load-bearing (it is what makes the corpus bugs trigger here) and doubles as documentation-by-example, the same way this repo's `project.portability` and `project.engine-layer-enforcement` entries demo the plan-review project overlay.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` — the `code_review.concurrency` project entry.\n- `docs/llm-framework.md` — the two-layer trigger section.\n- `tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py` — the config-effect case below (module created by the prerequisite trigger-extension story).\n\n## Testing\n\nConfig-effect contrast (shared test-design standard §6), unit tier: one test proving the project entry changes behavior through the REAL read path — a fixture diff containing `index.lock` (a project token deliberately absent from the committed high-precision list) fires the `concurrency` overlay when `content_triggered_overlays` runs with `repo_root` = this checkout, and does NOT fire under committed-only routing (no `repo_root` / a bare tmp root). A test that only parsed the JSON would miss the read-but-miswired class; this asserts the observable outcome differs across the two routing states.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` carries the `code_review.concurrency` entry with the tokens and globs listed in the Approach; checked by reading the file.\n- [ ] Config-effect test: a diff containing `index.lock` fires `concurrency` under this repo's effective routing and not under committed-only routing; checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_triggers.py`.\n- [ ] `docs/llm-framework.md` documents the two tiers, the fire-rate measurement, and how a project adds vocabulary, using this repo's entry as the worked example; checked by reading the section.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the targeted pytest module pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787263500005536001,"uuid":"421737db-ee53-45e8-b821-51ce1dc9ba4f"}